ll" ref="refs/changes/07/575007/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Iba3336a353e0c41e5ad0f8e187937a37dc3aaad8" commit="f75153667e1cdded0de157d02dc38e885b02e61b" commit_message="sys-devel/llvm: uprev llvm to 4.0.1 (reland)  We were expanding the ${SYSROOT} variable at build time instead of at runtime, leading to CQ failures.  When creating the llvm-config wrapper, some boards pointed to the wrong sysroot. For example, the llvm-config wrapper for reef pointed to:  /build/amd64-generic/usr/lib/llvm/bin/llvm-config  instead of  /build/reef/usr/lib/llvm/bin/llvm-config  Let's escape the ${SYSROOT} variable so it points to the correct place.  BUG=chromium:746968 TEST=emerge-reef llvm mesa  Change-Id: Iba3336a353e0c41e5ad0f8e187937a37dc3aaad8 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/580488 Commit-Ready: Gurchetan Singh &lt;gurchetansingh@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Gurchetan Singh &lt;gurchetansingh@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="2" gerrit_number="580488" owner_email="gurchetansingh@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/88/580488/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="2"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I87f6d59073a63bfe206cdc72bada95e696ba3dd1" commit="0bdd98cd9726fd2e639aeb0148beee606d524b82" commit_message="sys-devel/llvm: disable LLVM_ENABLE_TERMINFO  Mesa does not use it, and it causes build problems. For more information, see here:  http://marc.info/?l=llvm-dev&amp;m=138925000313756&amp;w=2  BUG=chromium:734282 TEST=emerge-cyan llvm mesa with CL:560629 works  Change-Id: I87f6d59073a63bfe206cdc72bada95e696ba3dd1 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/580337 Commit-Ready: Gurchetan Singh &lt;gurchetansingh@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Gurchetan Singh &lt;gurchetansingh@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Stéphane Marchesin &lt;marcheu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="2" gerrit_number="580337" owner_email="gurchetansingh@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/37/580337/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="2"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I5b0b28e5e19fe181fa6e58ad67b2f443934b8832" commit="45d1c8e6628fa92e3ef7902a695bbe5bac13984a" commit_message="power: Don't suspend console when using suspend_to_idle  Suspend_on_idle on Intel systems is intended to take the system into a low power state S0ix instead of S3.  Intel identified that not suspending the UART can cause failure to go into S0ix state (via suspend-to-idle) and thus rendering system stay in higher package power state such as PC10, because a non suspended UART can cause events that affect PMC's decision to enter S0ix. Hence this patch suspends the console when using Suspend-to- idle.  The suspend and resume kernel logs are still going to be available in dmesg / syslog. The logs will also eventually appear on the UART itself  (but will be printed after a delay because the messages are saved in a buffer and printed after the UART is resumed).  BUG=b:63737106 TEST=check that console is suspended when using S0ix, and not suspended when using S3.  Change-Id: I5b0b28e5e19fe181fa6e58ad67b2f443934b8832 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/580674 Commit-Ready: Rajat Jain &lt;rajatja@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Rajat Jain &lt;rajatja@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Dan Erat &lt;derat@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Rajneesh Bhardwaj &lt;rajneesh.bhardwaj@intel.com&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="580674" owner_email="rajatja@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.google